WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.670
In this week, you'll hear

2
00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.450
about sequence to
sequence models,

3
00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:09.250
which are useful
for everything from

4
00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.995
machine translation to
speech recognition.

5
00:00:11.995 --> 00:00:13.870
Let's start with
the basic models,

6
00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.495
and then later this week,
you'll hear about beam search,

7
00:00:16.495 --> 00:00:19.120
the attention model, and we
will wrap up the discussion

8
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.910
of models for audio
data like speech.

9
00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:26.340
Let's get started. Let's
say you want to input

10
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:31.014
a French sentence like Jane
visite I'Afrique Septembre,

11
00:00:31.014 --> 00:00:34.170
and you want to translate
it to the English sentence,

12
00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.695
Jane is visiting
Africa in September.

13
00:00:36.695 --> 00:00:40.830
As usual, let's use x1 through x,

14
00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.870
in this case 5 to represent

15
00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.960
the words and the input sequence,

16
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.810
and we'll use y1 through y6 to

17
00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:51.205
represent the words in
the output sequence.

18
00:00:51.205 --> 00:00:54.440
How can you train a
neural network to input

19
00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:58.435
the sequence x and
output the sequence y?

20
00:00:58.435 --> 00:01:00.875
Well, here's something
you could do.

21
00:01:00.875 --> 00:01:04.205
The ideas I'm about to
present are mainly from

22
00:01:04.205 --> 00:01:10.875
these two papers due
to Ilya Sutskever, Vinyals Oriol,
and V. Le. Quoc  and

23
00:01:10.875 --> 00:01:11.910
that one by

24
00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:22.020
Kyunghyun Cho, Bart van Merriënboer,
Caglar Gulcehre, Dzmitry Bahdanau,
Fethi Bougares, Holger Schwenk, and Yoshua Bengio.

25
00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.610
First, let's have a network
which we're going to call

26
00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:29.450
the encoder network be built as

27
00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:31.770
a RNN and this could be a

28
00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:36.050
Gru or LSTM feeding
the input French words

29
00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:37.645
one word at a time.

30
00:01:37.645 --> 00:01:42.900
After ingesting the
input sequence the RNN

31
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:48.110
then outputs a vector that
represents the input sentence.

32
00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:51.905
After that, you can
build a decoded network,

33
00:01:51.905 --> 00:01:53.285
which you might draw here.

34
00:01:53.285 --> 00:01:55.070
Which takes as input

35
00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:57.260
the encoding output by

36
00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.109
the encoding network shown
in black on the left,

37
00:02:00.109 --> 00:02:03.275
and then can be trained to output

38
00:02:03.275 --> 00:02:10.445
the translation one
word at a time.

39
00:02:10.445 --> 00:02:15.705
Eventually, it helps
us say the end of

40
00:02:15.705 --> 00:02:17.755
sequence and the sentence token

41
00:02:17.755 --> 00:02:21.140
upon which the decoder
stops, and as usual,

42
00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.570
we could take the
generator tokens

43
00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:26.200
and feed them to the next

44
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.160
so if they just stay in the
sequence they were doing

45
00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.815
before when synthesizing text
using the language model.

46
00:02:32.815 --> 00:02:35.340
One of the most
remarkable recent results

47
00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.445
in deep learning is
that this model works.

48
00:02:38.445 --> 00:02:42.210
Given enough pairs of French
and English sentences,

49
00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.315
if you train a model to input

50
00:02:45.315 --> 00:02:46.920
a French sentence and

51
00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.649
output the corresponding
English translation,

52
00:02:49.649 --> 00:02:52.395
this will actually
work decently well.

53
00:02:52.395 --> 00:02:56.580
This model simply uses an
encoding network whose job it

54
00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.300
is to find an encoding of
the input French sentence,

55
00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.180
and then use a decoding
network to then

56
00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:07.005
generate the corresponding
English translation.

57
00:03:07.005 --> 00:03:09.840
An architecture very similar to

58
00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.665
this also works for
image captioning.

59
00:03:13.665 --> 00:03:16.650
Given an image like
the one shown here,

60
00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.510
maybe you wanted to be captions

61
00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.855
automatically as a cat
sitting on a chair.

62
00:03:21.855 --> 00:03:23.970
How do you train
in your network to

63
00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.010
input an image and output

64
00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:30.900
a caption like that
phrase up there?

65
00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.150
Here's what you can do.

66
00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.820
From the earlier course
on the ConvNets,

67
00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.820
you've seen and how you can input

68
00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.820
an image into a
convolutional network,

69
00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.100
may maybe a pre-trained AlexNet,

70
00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.425
and have that learn and encoding

71
00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:48.905
a learner to the features
of the input image.

72
00:03:48.905 --> 00:03:52.395
This is actually the
AlexNet architecture,

73
00:03:52.395 --> 00:03:56.735
and if we get rid of
this final softmax unit,

74
00:03:56.735 --> 00:03:59.880
the pre-trained AlexNet
can give you a

75
00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.240
4,096-dimensional
feature vector of

76
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.590
which to represent
this picture of a cat.

77
00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:08.820
This pre-trained network can

78
00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.610
be the encoded network
for the image and you

79
00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:14.600
now have a 4,096-dimensional

80
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:17.420
vector that represents the image.

81
00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.690
You can then take
this and feed it to

82
00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:24.170
an RNN whose job it is to

83
00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:29.215
generate the caption
one word at a time.

84
00:04:29.215 --> 00:04:34.610
Similar to what we saw
with machine translation,

85
00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:36.515
translating from
French the English,

86
00:04:36.515 --> 00:04:38.150
you can now input

87
00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:42.830
a feature vector describing
the inputs and then

88
00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:49.335
have it generate an
output set of words,

89
00:04:49.335 --> 00:04:50.880
one word at a time.

90
00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.905
This actually works pretty
well for image captioning,

91
00:04:54.905 --> 00:04:56.450
especially if the
caption you want to

92
00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.630
generate is not too long.

93
00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:02.120
As far as I know, this type

94
00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:04.400
of model was first proposed by

95
00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:10.280
Jin Hwan Mau Wei Xu Yong Chung Wang, Xiao Hong and Alan Yo. although it turns

96
00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.590
out there are multiple
groups coming up with

97
00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:14.615
very similar models independently

98
00:05:14.615 --> 00:05:17.340
and at about the same time.

99
00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:20.980
Two of the groups that had done

100
00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:22.330
very similar work at

101
00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:24.490
about the same time and
I think independently of

102
00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:32.915
Junhua Mao, Wei Xu, Yi Yang, Jiang Wang, Zhiheng Huang,
Alan L. Yuille as well as Adrej
Karpathy and Fei-Fei Li.

103
00:05:32.915 --> 00:05:35.010
You've now seen how

104
00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:37.740
a basic sequence to
sequence model works.

105
00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:39.730
How basic image to sequence,

106
00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:41.890
or image captioning model works.

107
00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:43.750
But there are some
differences between

108
00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:45.970
how you'll run a model like this,

109
00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:49.135
the generally the sequence
compared to how you were

110
00:05:49.135 --> 00:05:52.920
synthesizing novel text
using a language model.

111
00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.190
One of the key differences
is you don't want

112
00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:57.385
to randomly choose
in translation.

113
00:05:57.385 --> 00:06:00.010
You may be want the
most likely translation

114
00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:02.140
or you don't want to
randomly choose in caption,

115
00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:03.730
maybe not, but you might want

116
00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:06.385
the best caption and
most likely caption.

117
00:06:06.385 --> 00:06:08.380
Let's see in the next video how

118
00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:11.180
you go about generating that.